4,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,2609,400,32,3,15448,14696,2878,476,389,279,9334,892,702,14002,23462,518,4930,15,11,15,15087,323,4930,17,11,17,8,12947,5126,400,33,3,15448,14696,2878,476,389,279,9334,892,702,14002,23462,518,3501,4930,19,11,17,15087,323,4930,20,11,18,8,12947,3555,374,279,12196,3204,897,315,279,30232,315,279,1555,8482,3501,400,32,3,323,400,33,3,30,17399,697,4226,438,264,4185,19419,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nPoint $A$ lies somewhere within or on the square which has opposite corners at $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$. Point $B$ lies somewhere within or on the square which has opposite corners at points $(4,2)$ and $(5,3)$. What is the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points $A$ and $B$? Express your answer as a common fraction.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"2df10a743a45f328892a832d19830caf1b392d784f6247a98b6a908fc356b6ed"},"seconds":13.126615762710571,"seed":893589370044944560,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we start by expressing the coordinates of points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Let \\(A = (x_1, y_1)\\) and \\(B = (x_2, y_2)\\). The slope \\(m\\) of the line through points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is given by:\n\\[\nm = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we identify the ranges for \\(x_1\\) and \\(y_1\\) for point \\(A\\). Since point \\(A\\) lies within the square with opposite corners at \\((0,0)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), we have:\n\\[\n0 \\leq x_1 \\leq 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 0 \\leq y_1 \\leq 2\n\\]\n\nNext, we identify the ranges for \\(x_2\\) and \\(y_2\\) for point \\(B\\). Since point \\(B\\) lies within the square with opposite corners at \\((4,2)\\) and \\((5,3)\\), we have:\n\\[\n4 \\leq x_2 \\leq 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2 \\leq y_2 \\leq 3\n\\]\n\nTo maximize the slope \\(m\\), we need to maximize the numerator \\(y_2 - y_1\\) and minimize the denominator \\(x_2 - x_1\\). The maximum value of \\(y_2 - y_1\\) occurs when \\(y_2 = 3\\) and \\(y_1 = 0\\), giving:\n\\[\ny_2 - y_1 = 3 - 0 = 3\n\\]\n\nThe minimum value of \\(x_2 - x_1\\) occurs when \\(x_2 = 4\\) and \\(x_1 = 2\\), giving:\n\\[\nx_2 - x_1 = 4 - 2 = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of the slope \\(m\\) is:\n\\[\nm = \\frac{3 - 0}{4 - 2} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:817"}
